WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.080
Series finales can be tough to pull off for
even the best TV shows.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:09.020
They have to wrap all of the show's plotlines
and character arcs in a way that is satisfactory

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:09.940
and sensical.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:15.140
Sometimes, that means a show will close on
a question mark to allow fans their own interpretations

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.600
or perhaps even room for a spin-off.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:23.220
And while that might work with some programs,
an open ended finale can often seem so unfinished

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.500
that fans are left clamoring for answers years
after the fact.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.159
To help resolve some of the most frustrating
show endings, here's a look at what showrunners

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.180
really meant for fans to take away from these
confusing conclusions.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:36.680
Lost

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:43.080
For a show that was already redefining weird,
Lost's last episode really went off the rails.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:47.840
The fifth season ended with Jack and the crew
setting off a hydrogen bomb on the island

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.540
while visiting the 1970's, so the sixth and
final season picked up with two separate timelines:

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.719
one where the survivors are still stuck on
the island in the present, and another where

00:00:57.719 --> 00:00:59.820
the plane seemingly never crashed.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.320
This alternate timeline soon revealed itself
to be much different than the original, and

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.040
many of the characters were living completely
different lives.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.280
The final episode revealed that this timeline
was actually the afterlife.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.900
Carlton Cuse and Damon Lindelof later explained
that the afterlife plotline was supposed to

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.700
demonstrate how the events on the island weren't
purgatory, as many suspected.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:27.420
Unfortunately, the message was so, well, lost
in the chaos of the sideways world exploration

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.420
that even they couldn't really explain all
the finite details.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.380
The Sopranos

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.780
The fade-to-black finish for The Sopranos
was so unexpected that many viewers thought

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.260
their cable feed had been interrupted when
it happened.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.620
Fans of the show had spent months and months
speculating about what incredible drama would

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:51.780
befall Tony Soprano and his family to close
out such an eventful show, but … none of

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:52.280
that happened.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:57.840
Instead, Tony, his wife Carmela, and their
kids met up for dinner -- after an excruciatingly

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.140
drawn-out parallel parking job by Meadow.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:05.580
With Journey's "Don't Stop Believing" blaring
out over all the inaction, Tony noticed a

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.220
suspicious man lurking around the diner and
...

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:09.900
"(Ding)"

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.040
"Don't stop"

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.840
According to series creator David Chase, the
ending implied that Tony was always going

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:23.010
to be paranoid as a result of his mafioso
history and that endings for guys like Tony

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.620
tend to happen just that quickly and unexpectedly.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:30.800
Thanks to some frame-by-frame analyses after
the fact, it's pretty clear that the guy Tony

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:35.739
was eyeing, who went to the bathroom nearby
his table, probably ended his life right then

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:36.240
and there.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:41.300
But the fact that Chase chose not to actually
show his death was irritating, considering

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:45.080
the show wasn't shy about showcasing some
serious brutality before.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.200
Parks and Recreation

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.970
The final season of Parks and Recreation jumped
forward in time a few years to 2017, in which

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:56.860
Leslie Knope has become a regional director
of the National Parks Service.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:01.760
But if that time-tripping wasn't enough, the
series finale skipped even further ahead to

00:03:01.769 --> 00:03:06.390
detail the next few years in the lives of
all the main characters, particularly Leslie

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.600
and her husband, fellow Pawnee government
worker Ben Wyatt.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:14.280
Both are approached to run for governor of
Indiana, with Leslie said to have served two

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:17.560
terms in the post, while Ben went on to serve
in Congress.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.690
"Joe Biden knows his way around a seafood
risotto."

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.580
Then, much farther along in the future, they
attend the funeral of Jerry Gergich with a

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.300
Secret Service detail in tow.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.900
That means either Leslie or Ben is President
of the United States, but who is it?

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:38.980
Show creator Mike Schur, inspired by the ambiguity
of the finale of The Sopranos, says he felt

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.880
"there was room for one big question mark."

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.450
Schur told Variety that he "wanted people
to be able to fill in their own blanks and

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:46.420
make up their own minds."

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:51.780
Considering the fact that Leslie Knope always
had Oval Office ambitions, it was probably

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:57.250
her, but the fact that we didn't get to find
out for sure meant fans had to order extra

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:58.560
waffles to cope.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:03:59.720
30 Rock

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:05.080
NBC's 30 Rock seemed to have a relatively
straightforward finale, going full meta by

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.700
having their faux show, TGS, wrap in conjunction
with the real show's end.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.300
But there was one element of the big finish
that was extremely odd.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:17.720
In the very last scene of the show, which
takes place several generations in the future,

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:22.100
Liz Lemon's granddaughter is all grown up
and is pitching a show about her grandmother

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:28.060
to the new head of the network, who just so
happens to be... an apparently immortal Kenneth?

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:33.220
Throughout the show, Kenneth was an NBC page
who was painted as something of a simpleton,

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:37.501
but in retrospect, the show might've been
building to the reveal that Kenneth was a

00:04:37.501 --> 00:04:39.260
timeless creature all along.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:44.140
After all, how many references did Kenneth
make to old actors and songs that were so

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:47.910
obscure even those in the entertainment business
around him couldn't keep up?

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:50.640
"I was pretty addicted to coke back in my
Wall Street days."

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:56.340
Mad Men

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:02.580
After nearly a decade of failed marriages,
salacious affairs, a bunch of booze, and a

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:08.670
deep dive into his abandoned identity, Donald
Draper escaped his life as a snazzy Madison

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:12.200
Avenue executive and sought solace in a hippie
commune.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:18.020
His journey to rock bottom was the very fabric
of Mad Men's seven seasons, so when he showed

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:23.600
up at a California hideaway desperately weeping
over his mistakes and emotional disconnections,

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.260
it seemed like Don might finally change his
ways.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:31.980
But that final shot of him peacefully meditating
en masse was not the end-all be-all for the

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.160
creative genius' days in the sun.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:39.630
The series ended with a credits cutaway to
the famous 1971 Coca-Cola commercial of a

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:43.990
cross-cultural singing group chanting about
wanting to buy the world a Coke and indicated

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:49.540
that Don's take away from so much self-discovery
was really just another awesome ad idea.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.520
Mad Men creator Matthew Weiner later agreed
that Don indeed remained an ad man to the

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.850
end, as the commercial suggested, saying,
"Why not end this show with the greatest commercial

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:01.350
ever made?

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:04.400
I am not [into] ambiguity for ambiguity's
sake.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.840
But it was nice to have your cake and eat
it too, in terms of what is advertising, who

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:10.880
is Don and what is that thing?"

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:16.120
As a result, viewers were left thirsty for
more, so the choice of a soda ad was certainly

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:16.620
inspired.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:17.940
Thanks for watching!

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:20.810
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:22.910
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

